WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.743
- [Narrator] In movies, cars
get blown up and flipped.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.763
They define characters or
they're characters themselves.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.320
Sometimes they're the center of the shot

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.020
and other times they're in the background

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.120
just helping to set the scene.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.953
But where do these cars come from?

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:26.990
Some of them come from the Auto Film Club

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.690
in Staten Island, New York.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:33.690
- We rent vehicles, we modify
vehicles, we acquire vehicles.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.490
- [Narrator] The Lucci family has been in

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.620
the car rental business for 43 years.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.140
Max's father Ralph had an
autobody shop with his brother

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:45.880
in Brooklyn and they got to
know people with old cars.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.850
- They started getting calls
from different prop masters

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.907
and people in the film industry saying,

00:00:52.907 --> 00:00:55.247
"Oh, you guys have a body shop

00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:56.887
where you have all these older cars.

00:00:56.887 --> 00:00:59.910
"I need cars for a period piece."

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.560
- [Narrator] After awhile,
they were acting as

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.810
liaisons between film
crews and car owners.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.540
Eventually they started buying cars

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.750
to keep in their own inventory as well.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.650
Now, they've got about 190
vehicles available on their lots

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.922
and a database full of car
owners willing to rent.

00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:18.260
(engine revving)

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.160
The cars they have range from flashy,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.730
polished collectibles to beat-up junkers.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.790
- Whether it's your pristine garage queen

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.740
from the '60s or '70s or your jalopy,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.350
you know, your Subaru Outback,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.120
whatever it may be
there's always a character

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.470
who needs a specific type of car.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.850
- [Narrator] The way it usually works is

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.290
a production company calls with

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.890
a request for a particular type of car.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.190
If the Auto Film Club
doesn't have the car,

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.560
they'll act as the agent
for someone who does

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.040
or try to find one to buy.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.310
If they do have the car, they'll bring it

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.660
into the garage and make sure it's

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.773
running well before it has to be on set.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.220
- If there needs to be a shot where a car

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.500
is having a hard brake and it stops

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.740
right in front of the
principal actor or actress,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.180
then we'll inspect the
vehicle, check out the brakes.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.260
- [Narrator] This is all part of

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.670
getting the car stunt ready.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.170
They might also prepare
a car to burn rubber,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:15.060
be smashed, or get flipped.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.150
For the movie This Is Where I Leave You,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.680
the Auto Film Club had to find

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.210
an old Jaguar and then gut it.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.690
- We removed the motor and
the transmission from it,

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.260
made it hollow, and
then we had some people

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.700
from special effects come in and fabricate

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.950
a fake-looking motor that
we put into the vehicle

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.510
and then we had to weld
a pipe so that the actors

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.710
who were flipping over this
real car could use it to

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.033
gain leverage and actually
flip over this old Jaguar.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.550
- [Narrator] You might not know it,

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.850
but you've probably seen some
of Auto Film Club's cars.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.110
They supplied about 14 cabs for this scene

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.340
from the 2002 Spider-Man movie.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.203
The rest you see were added
digitally in post-production.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.070
They supplied the cars for Men in Black,

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.860
including the Zap-Em van
and the car that Will Smith

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.420
smashes through when he
shoots the cricket gun.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.910
That one was the Lucci family's
car when Max was growing up.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.600
- It used to get driven
to school, kindergarten,

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.960
every single day, we called it Old Betsy.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.840
- [Narrator] Auto Film Club
has supplied cars for Gotham,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.830
the Americans, Jessica
Jones, and the list goes on.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.310
- You can never say no
to a potential client

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:29.977
because you never know when
they're gonna call you back.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.800
He told me to call this
person who told me to

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.480
call that guy, who knows a guy in Wyoming.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.377
I finally called someone and I said,

00:03:38.377 --> 00:03:43.377
"Do you have a 1993 to
'97 Prevost bus hatch?"

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.637
And he said, "Yeah, I got
one right in my yard."

